Q: Regular expression to match standard 10 digit phone number A: <p>How about this?</p>
<pre><code>^(\+?[01])?[-.\s]?\(?[1-9]\d{2}\)?[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}
</code></pre>

Q: Getting the actual length of a UTF-8 encoded std::string? A: <p>If you want to know the count of <strong>Unicode code-points</strong>, then use <code>mbsrtowcs()</code> with a null destination.  This is specified to return the number of characters which would have been converted were the destination not null.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;wchar.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

/* Count Unicode codepoints in multibyte string */
/* If mbstr is not a valid multibyte sequence, returns (size_t)-1 */
size_t count_codepoints(const char* mbstr)
{
    mbstate_t state;
    memset(&amp;state, 0, sizeof state);
    return mbsrtowcs(NULL, &amp;mbstr, 0, &amp;state);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Demo:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;locale.h&gt;

int main(void)
{
    setlocale(LC_ALL, &quot;C.utf8&quot;);
    static const char *s = u8&quot;z\u00df\u6c34\U0001f34c&quot;;
    wprintf(L&quot;\&quot;%s\&quot; has %zu code-points.\n&quot;
            ,    s,       count_codepoints(s));
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;zß水🍌&quot; has 4 code-points.
</code></pre>
